The Epoc covers the AF3, F7, F3, FC5, T7, P7, O1, O2, P8, T8, FC6, F4, F8, AF4  channels, described below: 

| Channels | Nearby region | Functions associated with that region |
|----------|---------------|---------------------------------------|
| **AF3, AF4** | Prefrontal| Attention, working memory, planning and decision-making |
| **F3, F4**   | Frontal   | Working memory, executive control and aspects of movement planning |
| **F7, F8**   | Lateral frontal | Language-related processing, response inhibition and cognitive control |
| **FC5, FC6** | Lateral frontocentral | Movement preparation and planning; aspects of speech and sensorimotor processing |
| **T7, T8**   | Temporal | Auditory processing, aspects of language and memory |
| **P7, P8**   | Posterior parietal / temporoparietal | Sensory integration, spatial attention and aspects of body representation |
| **O1, O2**   | Occipital | Visual processing |


Missing are the C3, Cz, and C4 channels, which cover sensorimotor region, where the movement desicions are made, called Motor Imagery (MI).
these channels are missing from the Epoc X, which is a main limitation as mentioned by  (Tarara et al., 2025)     

A primary limitation contributing to this lack of differentiation is the 
absence of central electrodes in the Emotiv EPOC X, 
which impedes the precise capture of signal changes associated with MI. 
This limitation highlights the importance of utilizing equipment that can 
provide more comprehensive coverage of the motor cortex.

Despite these findings, the approach of combining these specific movements has not been 
previously explored, suggesting a potential avenue for future research. It is recommended to investigate whether alternative EEG headsets 
equipped with central electrodes can provide better performance for this type of BCI application, thus facilitating a more nuanced analysis of
MI and expanding the capabilities of BCIs in practical settings.




The concept behind decoding EEG signals is the focus on the power of the mu and beta signals , which have the frequency ranges of 8-12 Hz and 12-20 Hz respectivly. when looking into the power drops of these 
signals over repetative "motion" doing in our case, we can use a model to train it into memorizing this signal and what it means.
In our case we may shift to collect data using the Language or wording imagination, which would be classified for the model as Move your arm up and down, where the data collection and model pipeline are the same for all types of functions. 


